In every corner of the street,  
Where bustling feet and echoes meet,  
Lies a haven, humble and discreet,  
A shelter from life’s stormy sheet.

Its doors are open, arms wide spread,  
For weary souls with dreams unfed.  
A quilt of warmth, a place to rest,  
In troubled times, it's a humble nest.

The walls may lack in gilt and shine,  
Yet offer more than gold's own line.  
They speak in whispers, soft and kind,  
With stories woven into their bind.

No judgment here, no glance askew,  
Only hearts that mend, hearts that renew.  
With every cup of tea that's shared,  
A token of hope, profoundly declared.

The shelters cradle the lost and stray,  
Nurturing spirits, come what may.  
They gift the wandering a chance to be,  
To bloom anew, to set them free.

For within these halls, compassion reigns,  
Healing wounds, easing pains.  
A bastion bright, a linchpin strong,  
A gentle lullaby, a hopeful song.

So let us cherish these sacred spaces,  
These guardians graced with kind embraces.  
For in a world so wild and vast,  
Our humble shelters hold steadfast.

