**Title: Fathers' Day**

**Scene: A modest living room with worn but comfortable furniture. A window reveals a sunny afternoon outside. A small table is set with a simple lunch. JACK, an energetic man in his mid-30s, busily arranges sandwiches while his father, HENRY, a gentle but reserved man in his 60s, sits nearby with a newspaper.**

---

**JACK**  
(Placing the last plate down) There we go, Dad—turkey sandwiches, just the way you like 'em.

**HENRY**  
(Lowering the newspaper and smiling) You remembered the mustard.

**JACK**  
Of course. I wouldn’t dare forget, not after last time.

**HENRY**  
(Nods, chuckling) It’s funny, isn’t it? The little things that stick. Like when you were a kid and you’d only eat your crusts if there was that specific jam, remember?

**JACK**  
With the little strawberries on the label. Yeah, I remember. Mom would buy it in bulk just for me.

**HENRY**  
(Looks away, smiling to himself) She had a way of knowing what everyone needed.

**JACK**  
(Sitting down across from HENRY) And so do you, Dad. You always seemed to know exactly what to say when I was struggling with something, even if I didn’t quite understand it then.

**HENRY**  
(Quietly) I guess... I just hoped you’d remember I was there for you, even when I wasn’t sure myself.

**JACK**  
I know. And that means a lot. You’ve done more than enough, Dad. (Pauses, looking thoughtful) It's strange, isn't it? Being here, on a day like this, just the two of us.

**HENRY**  
(Takes a deep breath) Strange, yes. But nice. It’s nice to have this time with you, Jack.

**JACK**  
(Reaches across the table, placing his hand on top of HENRY’s) I’m glad we took today to ourselves. Sometimes I think it’s easy to forget the bond we share—the little things and the big ones.

**HENRY**  
(Softly) Like sitting in silence, knowing that’s enough. It’s something I wished I realized sooner with my own father.

**JACK**  
(Smiles warmly) And now we have the chance to get it right with each other.

**HENRY**  
(Nods, visibly moved) Yes, yes we do.

---

**(They sit quietly for a moment, sharing a comfortable silence as the sunlight begins to cast warm patterns across the room.)**

---

**END SCENE**

